Pins that a python open(p,'w') call whose path argument is a bare identifier is not denied outright as non-literal when that identifier was assigned a plain quoted-string literal earlier in the same snippet (p='target.txt') -- the guard resolves the identifier to its last preceding single-line plain-string assignment before the open( call and runs the normal protected-file check against that resolved literal, denying with the accurate overwrite reason (naming target.txt, not the variable name p).
